  "name": "Catalan sardana (national-folk circle-dance)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Catalan sardana circle-dance-and-music tradition of Catalonia (Spain), the canonical Catalan national-folk-dance form. Performance configuration: a cobla ensemble of eleven musicians playing tenora (Catalan oboe), tible (smaller oboe), trumpets, fiscorn (flugelhorn-like brass), trombone, contrabass, and the flabiol-and-tambori one-handed flute-and-drum combination played simultaneously by a single musician. Dancers stand in a circle holding hands, performing the precise counting-of-steps Catalan choreographic vocabulary. The sardana became politically significant during the Franco-period (1939-1975) suppression of Catalan culture, and post-1975 democratic-Catalonia consolidation. Distinguished from other Iberian circle-dance forms by sardana's eleven-instrument-cobla and precise-counted-step structural conventions.",
